00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:04.760 align:start position:0%
viii assertive isms existentialism there  they are  the quintessential existentialists  lounging in a Parisian left-bank cafe  discussing the meaninglessness of life  there is some truth behind every detail  of this stereotype but in its totality  it's a misleading caricature he knows  much too jean-paul Sartre and Albert  Camus both philosophers writers and  famous public intellectuals in France  from the Second World War  until Arthur's death in 1980 South  argued that the fundamental principle of  existentialism is that existence  precedes essence in other words we have  to create our own lives our own selves  our own values God does not exist  we are born without a destiny it's all  up to us we are therefore condemned to  be free condemned because our freedom is  far from that of the carefree hedonistic  consumed on a shopping spree we have an  absolute responsibility for ourselves  which can lead to despair and anguish  even questioning the point of living a  classic existential crisis this  existential angst has not gone away it's  the human condition as real today as  ever political freedom and material  prosperity and the struggle for basic  existence and survival but only increase  the struggle to find genuine meaning for  our existence why are we here how can we  make a difference how should we really  live southla took our freedom is given  but his partner and fellow philosopher  Simone de Beauvoir pointed out this not  always that simple in her seminal book  the second sex she argued the society  and political structures often conspire  to hold women back Camus argued that yes  we are free but the world itself is  absurd he be told the classical myth of  Sisyphus condemned ever to push a rock  up a hill only to see it roll back down  to him to start all over again like  Sisyphus our task is to accept our own  absurd lives cheerfully wonderful though  life can be it has no ultimate purpose  and we all end as dust however not all  existentialists believe we live in a  godless senseless universe  the father of existentialism the 19th  century Dame søren kierkegaard believed  in the Christian God but argued reason  and evidence can never prove that he  exists we have to take a non rational  leap of faith at its heart then  existentialism is not really all about  meaninglessness it's about the  responsibility we all have to make the  right choices in life both for ourselves  and for others a heavy responsibility  indeed and just as important now as in  post-war Paris
 

